<description>
Mirror colored sprites using red (2) markers as reflection axes.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Red (2) = marker color.
- Colored sprites appear near red markers.

Rule:
- Red line within a sprite (vertical or horizontal) → mirror payload across that axis.
- Single red pixel in a mixed sprite → point-reflect payload around it.
- Isolated red pixel (alone, no payload) → point-reflect each adjacent 8-connected color component.
- Originals remain; mirrored pixels overlay.

Key insight: Red marker geometry (line vs single pixel) determines reflection type; isolated vs embedded red determines what gets reflected.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="eval" puzzle_id="8b9c3697" lines="9-23">
<description>
Insert red components into interior sockets of large structures, leaving a black trail.

Input structure:
- bg = most common. Large non-bg, non-red structures with interior cavities.
- Red components outside structures.

Rule:
- Interior sockets: bg cells inside a structure's bbox, adjacent to structure pixels.
- For each structure, find the direction where rays from sockets hit a red component.
- Slide that component into the socket; paint traversed cells black.
- Unmatched reds vanish.

Key insight: Docking direction is inferred by ray-casting from interior sockets to the nearest red component.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="6350f1f4" lines="62-81">
<description>
Decode a block-grid image by swapping each sprite-coded cell with a solid cell and each blank cell with the sprite.

Input structure
The grid is partitioned by black separator rows and columns into equal-size
square blocks. Each block is either a copy of one two-color sprite or a
uniform blank block of the sprite's minority color; grey pixels are erased
cells that act as wildcards.

Rule
Infer the repeated sprite from fully visible mixed-color blocks. Replace a
block matching that sprite, ignoring grey erasures, with a solid block of
the sprite's majority color. Replace a uniform blank block with the sprite.
Separator rows and columns, including grey erasures on them, become black.

Key insight
The small sprite is a block-level code for one macro-cell state, and the
uniform minority-color block is the other state; the output renders the
opposite code in each macro-cell while cleaning erased pixels.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="247ef758" lines="35-46">
<description>
Move colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.

Input structure:
- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.

Rule:
- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.
- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.

Key insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="15660dd6" lines="33-54">
<description>
Combine each column's repeated template mask with its single solid color swatch.

Input structure:
- The grid is a table of framed panels separated by background rows/columns.
- The leftmost column gives one solid row-label color for each panel row.
- In each panel column, exactly one panel has a solid non-background interior,
  while the other panels share the same mask drawn in one template color on
  the background.

Rule:
- For every panel column, find the row containing the solid interior swatch.
- Rebuild one output panel using that row's left label as the frame color.
- Fill the repeated template mask with the swatch color and leave the mask
  background cells as background; separate rebuilt panels with one
  background column.

Key insight:
The table encodes a colorized stencil per column: the solid panel supplies
the paint color and frame label, while the duplicate template panels supply
the shape mask.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="fc4aaf52" lines="17-34">
<description>
Split the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.

Input structure:
- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.
- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.
- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.

Rule:
- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.
- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.
- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left
  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.

Key insight:
- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,
  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="b99e7126" lines="34-48">
<description>
Scale a minority cell's inner pattern up to the cell-grid level, completing a fractal overlay.

Input structure:
- Grid divided into NxN cells by separator lines of a single color.
- Most cells share an identical interior pattern (majority); a few have a distinct pattern (minority).

Rule:
- Extract the inner pixel pattern from the minority cell.
- Treat each pixel as a cell-sized stamp: active pixels → minority pattern, inactive → majority pattern.
- Position this scaled-up pattern on the cell grid so existing minority cells align with active pixels.
- Fill in all remaining active positions with the minority pattern.

Key insight: The minority cell's interior is a miniature blueprint of where minority cells should appear at the grid scale — fractal self-similarity.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="ad173014" lines="97-111">
<description>
Recolor each framed interior mask with the color from the next red frame clockwise around the blue connector cycle.

Input structure
- The grid contains hollow red square frames connected pairwise by blue line components.
- The blue components connect the frames into one cycle, with every frame touching exactly two blue components.
- Each frame interior contains background plus exactly one non-background color mask.

Rule
- Sort the red frames clockwise by their centers around the cycle.
- Keep each frame's interior mask shape in place, but recolor it with the interior color from the next clockwise frame.
- Leave the red frames, blue connectors, background, and interior background holes unchanged.

Key insight
- The colored masks do not move; the blue cycle gives the order in which their colors rotate.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="f560132c" lines="259-273">
